teresting and enjoyable and I think  that people who are just getting  introduced to stoicism this could be  actually a good adjunct to reading some  of the other literature that's out there  because precisely because of its its  political dimension that it brings to  the fore so those are the good points  about the work  the aspects of the work that I find  problematic are pretty minor I would say  they might even be viewed by some as  merely quibbles but but I do think that  there are a few inaccuracies in some of  the story that that's being told and  it's not that surprising to me because  what we're dealing with here is kind of  a complicated intellectual history I  don't think that that McGeehan gets  Stoicism wrong as such but I think that  he gets some of the history of ideas  stuff wrong or or he just doesn't supply  things that they would be kind of useful  so you know for example he tells us that  the Stoics he essentially says basically  that the Stoics are coming up with the  cardinal virtues they divided virtues  into categories building the ladder  Christian framework for the four  cardinal virtues really I think the  plate nests deserve the the credit for  that I would say that the four cardinal  virtues I mean first of all Plato talks  about the four cardinal virtues right  you know Socrates have talked about  these but I think that historically  while stoicism did have an influence on  Christianity I think Neoplatonism  Platonism had a greater influence in  that respect there's kind of a strange  assertion about about Aristotelian ISM  saying that the Stoics are very much  Aristotelian asserting reality can be  determined through experience you know  Aristotle does have that that sort of  stance but this is not there this is not  where they actually do line up because  what they view is experience is quite  different again that's that's kind of an  epistemological quibble but but I do  think it's coming from somewhere I don't  really buy into the story that he's  telling about Plato and Aristotle and  stoicism in terms of politics and I'll  read a little bit of this Plato and  Aristotle had established political  theories stuck in the collectivist  Quagmire of the polis or Greek  city-state unable to move past their  status environment little distinction  was made between state and society  molded together the lack of separation  generally equated the two whereas the  state became society diverging from  these models stoicism treatment of the  individual enable the new way of thought  helping to throw off the mental shackles  of the state so that's a nice contrast  but I don't think that it really holds  up if you if you read Aristotle you know  read the politics read the rhetoric read  the nick of McCain ethics and I don't  really I don't really think it holds up  for for Plato as well I mean Aristotle  actually says none of the existing you  know constitutions meaning the  particular city-states actually are well  well structured here's how we might make  it better he gives you you know lots of  guidelines and then you know there's  there's kind of a claim a little bit  further on that the Stoics are really  the origin of the natural law tradition  and they are one major origin but so is  Aristotle again in the politics the  ethics and the rhetoric you you find  these discussions there which people  like Cicero would help to bring together  so I think that's a bit of a issue the  chronology I think was you know kind of  a good feature to have in there but  unfortunately it leaves off after  cruciferous succeeding clay anthems with  the the the history of the Stoics and it  doesn't talk about how the next goal  arcs are actually the ones to bring  stoic philosophy to Rome and and how you  know the Romans got introduced to it so  I think that's you know kind of a  problem there it's all about Roman  history at that point but but nothing  about the intersection with with  stoicism and then the modern references  like I mentioned there's about 20 pages  of references at the end you know it  just seems weird to me to include Thomas  Aquinas or Dante as modern references I  understand that that's opposed to  classical references and it you know I  would say there's just a lot of a lot of  books here and I don't know how useful  it is because none of its annotated so I  don't see that it's particularly helpful  but that's pretty much  so my parting thoughts on this book on  the whole I'd recommend it I think that  it's it's a good book to at least read  through if you're not particularly  interested in political dimensions  stoicism oven you might want to skip it  but if you are interested in the  intersection between stoicism and  society and politics particularly in the  present then I think you definitely want  to get this book you know the last thing  that I'll mention is I have you know a  much wider audience than just an  American audience and what we're talking  about here is an American legislator in  an American state talking about how  stoicism might fit into the system that  he's working in but but I think that the  principles that he's discussing at the  end would be useful for for anybody who  is thinking about you know a principled  way of doing politics in whatever system  they find themselves so it's not really  limited to just an American audience and  so on the whole I recommend the work and  I'd say I enjoyed it quite a bit  you
 

